      "body": "In commit \"rpc: handle malformed jsonrpc 2.0 requests correctly\" (02380237ddd2ed6878d7a3b0ae2807cfcf2cd9ff)\n\nSince this commit only adds test cases, it's hard to tell from the diff what behavior it changes. Could the commit message say what these requests did before? The PR description explains it, but the commit only has a subject line. Something like:\n\n```\nA JSON-RPC 2.0 request that fails to parse after its \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\" field\nhas been read, for example one with a missing or non-string method or\ninvalid params, reached the Assume in JSONErrorReply(). Debug builds\naborted, and release builds returned HTTP 400 with a 2.0 error body.\n\nReturn these errors like any other 2.0 error instead: HTTP 200 with the\nerror in the reply, or HTTP 204 and no reply for a request without an id.\n```\n\nThe test comments could explain more too. The [notification case](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/blob/02380237ddd2ed6878d7a3b0ae2807cfcf2cd9ff/test/functional/interface_rpc.py#L236-L239) confused me most: it wasn't clear why `{\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\"}` fails to parse (it has no `method`), or how this differs from the invalid-notification case just above it, which parses fine and fails when the method runs. Also, since this case doesn't follow the JSON-RPC 2.0 spec (see the [separate comment about it](https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/pull/36758#discussion_r4472663654)), the test should say so, so the behavior isn't mistaken for what the spec requires. Maybe:\n\n```diff\n--- a/test/functional/interface_rpc.py\n+++ b/test/functional/interface_rpc.py\n@@ -205,7 +205,8 @@ class RPCInterfaceTest(BitcoinTestFramework):\n         response, status = send_json_rpc(self.nodes[0], {\"jsonrpc\": \"3.0\", \"method\": \"getblockcount\"})\n         assert_equal(response, {\"result\": None, \"error\": {\"code\": RPC_INVALID_REQUEST, \"message\": \"JSON-RPC version not supported\"}})\n         assert_equal(status, 400)\n-        # invalid requests that are known to be 2.0 get RPC errors but not HTTP errors\n+        # Malformed requests that are known to be JSON-RPC 2.0, because they\n+        # have \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\", get RPC errors with HTTP 200, not HTTP errors.\n         for request, message in [\n             ({}, \"Missing method\"),\n             ({\"method\": 1}, \"Method must be a string\"),\n@@ -233,7 +234,11 @@ class RPCInterfaceTest(BitcoinTestFramework):\n         expect_http_rpc_status(204, None, self.nodes[0], \"generatetoaddress\", [1, \"invalid_address\"], 2, True)\n         # Sanity check: command was not executed\n         assert_equal(block_count + 1, self.nodes[0].getblockcount())\n-        # No error response for notifications that fail to parse either\n+        # No response either for a request without an id that fails to parse,\n+        # here because it has no method, so the error happens before any method\n+        # runs. This does not follow the JSON-RPC 2.0 spec, which treats an\n+        # invalid request as not being a notification and replies with an error\n+        # and \"id\": null.\n         response, status = send_json_rpc(self.nodes[0], {\"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\"})\n         assert_equal(response, None)\n         assert_equal(status, 204)\n```",
